# Domain & Application Architecture

## Target shape
Start as a modular monolith with explicit bounded contexts:
- Identity & Access
- Tenancy & Tenant Provisioning
- Customers
- Billing & Payments
- Commission / Wallet / Payout
- Agents
- Knowledge
- Telephony / Number Inventory
- Calls / Usage / Artifacts
- Integrations / Webhooks
- KYC / Risk
- Notifications
- Audit / Compliance
- Platform Configuration

## Layering
- Domain: invariants, policies, state machines, value objects.
- Application: use cases/orchestration, transaction boundaries, authorization context.
- Infrastructure: ORM repositories, providers, queues, storage, HTTP clients.
- API: serializers/schemas/controllers/views, validation, pagination, error mapping.
- Frontend: presentation/state management only; never authoritative business rules.

## Forbidden shortcuts
- ORM models must not become the only place where business workflows live.
- Domain/application code must not import provider SDKs directly when an adapter boundary is appropriate.
- Controllers must not contain financial calculations or complex tenancy routing.
- React must not decide authorization, pricing, entitlements, or tenant access.
- Avoid global mutable singleton state that carries tenant identity.

## Repository discipline
Keep modules cohesive. New cross-context dependencies require an explicit reason and should point toward a domain interface, application service, event or adapter rather than direct database coupling.
